# The Pythagorean triples, the whole-number right triangles, and their complete parametrization, proved as a universal
# ring identity and checked by `seed test`. Euclid's formula says that for any two integers m and n, the triple
# (m^2 - n^2, 2 m n, m^2 + n^2) satisfies the Pythagorean relation, and every primitive triple arises this way. The
# identity is that the first leg squared plus the second leg squared equals the hypotenuse squared, for ALL m and n.

# Euclid's parametrization: (m^2 - n^2)^2 + (2 m n)^2 = (m^2 + n^2)^2, for every m and n. So every choice of m and n
# yields a Pythagorean triple, and the squares of the two legs sum to the square of the hypotenuse.
rule euclid-s-formula-yields-pythagorean-triple
  seat m, like integer
  seat n, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add
        call multiply
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read m
              read m
            call multiply
              read n
              read n
          call subtract
            call multiply
              read m
              read m
            call multiply
              read n
              read n
        call multiply
          call multiply
            code 2
            call multiply
              read m
              read n
          call multiply
            code 2
            call multiply
              read m
              read n
      call multiply
        call add
          call multiply
            read m
            read m
          call multiply
            read n
            read n
        call add
          call multiply
            read m
            read m
          call multiply
            read n
            read n
